# =============================================================================
# .gitignore
#
# ~/.config is a real directory. Only the files listed below are linked back
# into it from this repo, one by one, by
# nix-darwin/modules/home-manager/xdg.nix.
#
# Never make ~/.config a single symlink to this repo's .config/. Every
# application then writes its runtime state inside the repo, VM images and all,
# and one `git add -A` publishes whatever tokens or session state those apps
# dropped there.
#
# .config/ uses an ALLOW-LIST rather than a deny-list. The per-file links are
# the first guard, but the linked paths *are* written to (VS Code rewrites
# settings.json, lazy.nvim writes lazy-lock.json), so the allow-list is the
# cheapest way to be sure only intended files are ever committed.
#
# To track a new config file:
#   1. add a `!/.config/path/to/file` line in the allow-list below
#   2. `git add .config/path/to/file`
# `git check-ignore -v <path>` explains why any path is ignored.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .config: deny everything
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
/.config/**

# Let git descend into directories so the allow-list below can match.
!/.config/**/

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .config: allow-list
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# VS Code
!/.config/Code/User/keybindings.json
!/.config/Code/User/settings.json

# Editors
!/.config/nvim/**/*.lua
# lazy.nvim writes its plugin lockfile into the config dir; it pins versions
# and belongs in git.
!/.config/nvim/lazy-lock.json
# lua_ls settings for editors that do not run init.lua to get them.
!/.config/nvim/.luarc.json
# `zg` (add the word under the cursor to the spell dictionary) writes here,
# because ~/.config/nvim is the first writable directory on the runtimepath.
# Only the word list: the .spl next to it is compiled from this file, so it is
# re-ignored here rather than in the runtime-dirs section below. Keep it here: a
# deny on the whole spell/ directory down there comes after this line and wins,
# which makes the allow-list entry above unreachable.
!/.config/nvim/spell/*.add
/.config/nvim/spell/*.spl

# Tooling
!/.config/gh/config.yml
!/.config/gh/hosts.yml

# Ghostty. Only the config: Ghostty keeps no state beside it, but a directory
# link would still pick up anything a future version decides to write there.
!/.config/ghostty/config

# Zellij. Only config.kdl: layouts/ and themes/ beside it are not ours, and the
# file itself is rewritten in place by zellij on a version upgrade, so an
# upgrade shows up as a diff here.
!/.config/zellij/config.kdl

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .config: re-ignore heavy runtime dirs
#
# These hold VM images and caches, which run to gigabytes. Listing them after
# the allow-list stops git from traversing them on every `status`.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
/.config/colima/
/.config/raycast/
/.config/opencode/
/.config/Transmission Remote GUI/
/.config/nvim/.netrwhist
/.config/photo-tagger/logs/

# =============================================================================
# Everything outside .config
# =============================================================================

# Build artifacts / nix
result
result-*
/nix-darwin/result
/nix-darwin/result-*

# Editor & OS noise
*.pyc
__pycache__/
.DS_Store
*.swp
*.swo
*~

# Linter caches. There is no Python project here, but ruff runs over the repo
# from the editor and drops one in the root. It stays out of `git status` on its
# own, because ruff writes a .gitignore inside it, which is a quiet way to be
# invisible rather than a reason to be here.
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/

# Secrets / local overrides (never commit)
*.env
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
!*.pub
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
*.kdbx
.netrc
.authinfo*

# AI tooling
/.github/instructions/codacy.instructions.md
